            <ArticleTitle>[Changes in the continuous electrocardiogram during the exercise stress test in women with angina pectoris and normal coronarography findings].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>259-62</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Ischaemic cardiac disease is usually diagnosed in patients with obstruent coronary arteries. However, it is also found in subjects with normal coronary arteries. Ten female patients, aged 30-40 years, with angina pectoris and normal coronary findings, were studied. Electrocardiographic changes were studied during physical tests and 24-hour electrocardiography. The findings suggested a mixed form of angina pectoris with both vasospasm and obstruent prearterioles.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
